---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<ToolLayout
  slug="reverse-dns"
  title="Reverse DNS Lookup"
  description="Look up the PTR record for any IPv4 or IPv6 address and check forward-confirmed reverse DNS — the match mail servers require before accepting mail."
  heading="Reverse DNS (PTR) lookup"
  intro="Enter an IPv4 or IPv6 address to see its PTR record, then have that hostname checked back against the address. This round trip — forward-confirmed reverse DNS — is what receiving mail servers actually test before they accept a message."
>
  <div slot="input" class="rdns-bar">
    <form class="rdns-form" id="rdns-form" novalidate>
      <label class="visually-hidden" for="rdns-input">IP address or hostname</label>
      <input
        id="rdns-input"
        class="rdns-input mono"
        type="text"
        autocomplete="off"
        autocapitalize="off"
        spellcheck="false"
        placeholder="8.8.4.4  ·  2001:4860:4860::8888  ·  mail.example.com"
      />
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary" id="rdns-go">
        Look up <Icon name="arrow" size={16} />
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost" id="rdns-mine">Use my IP</button>
    </form>

    <p class="rdns-examples">
      <span>Try</span>
      <button type="button" class="rdns-chip mono" data-ip="8.8.4.4">8.8.4.4</button>
      <button type="button" class="rdns-chip mono" data-ip="2001:4860:4860::8888">2001:4860:4860::8888</button>
      <button type="button" class="rdns-chip mono" data-ip="185.199.108.153">185.199.108.153</button>
      <button type="button" class="rdns-chip mono" data-ip="mail.protonmail.ch">mail.protonmail.ch</button>
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="section rdns-sec">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="rdns-out" id="rdns-out" aria-live="polite">
        <p class="rdns-idle">
          Results appear here. Both the reverse name and the forward-confirmation check run in your
          browser against Cloudflare's public DNS-over-HTTPS resolver — nothing is sent to us.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section rdns-notes-sec">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reverse DNS in practice</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Three things that trip people up</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Reverse DNS is the one record most people cannot publish themselves, and the one mail
          servers judge them on hardest. Here is the part that matters.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="glass-card">
          <div class="icon-tile"><Icon name="route" size={20} /></div>
          <h3 class="h3">The PTR record is not in your zone</h3>
          <p>
            Forward DNS follows names, reverse DNS follows address allocation. The record for
            <span class="mono">8.8.4.4</span> is called
            <span class="mono">4.4.8.8.in-addr.arpa</span>, and authority for that name is delegated
            down the same path the IP block was: IANA to a regional registry, registry to your ISP or
            hosting provider, and only then — sometimes — to you.
          </p>
          <p>
            So you set reverse DNS in your VPS provider's control panel, on the Elastic IP or
            instance that holds the address, or by asking your ISP. Adding a PTR-shaped record to
            <span class="mono">yourdomain.com</span>'s zone file does nothing at all: nobody ever
            queries that name. The lookup above shows which zone actually answers and which
            nameserver is primary for it, which is usually enough to tell you who to ask.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="glass-card">
          <div class="icon-tile"><Icon name="mail" size={20} /></div>
          <h3 class="h3">Why home connections cannot send mail</h3>
          <p>
            Residential ISPs assign generic reverse names across whole address pools —
            <span class="mono">host86-128-1-1.range86-128.example-isp.net</span> — and those names
            are formulaic on purpose. Receiving servers read the embedded octets and the
            <span class="mono">dsl</span>, <span class="mono">dyn</span>,
            <span class="mono">cable</span> or <span class="mono">pool</span> tokens as evidence that
            the address is a dynamically assigned end-user line, not a mail server.
          </p>
          <p>
            That reverse name is often technically valid and will even pass the confirmation check
            above, yet the mail is still refused: the same ranges are listed on Spamhaus's Policy
            Block List, and most ISPs block outbound port 25 outright. Passing FCrDNS with a generic
            name is necessary, not sufficient — you need an address whose reverse name you control.
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="glass-card">
          <div class="icon-tile"><Icon name="shield" size={20} /></div>
          <h3 class="h3">What confirmation proves</h3>
          <p>
            A forward-confirmed pair proves that whoever controls the reverse zone for the IP and
            whoever controls the forward zone for the hostname agree with each other. Since those are
            usually two different parties, agreement is hard to fake from a hijacked address or a
            botnet host, which is exactly why receivers like the signal.
          </p>
          <p>
            It proves nothing about the message. It does not authenticate the envelope sender or the
            <span class="mono">From:</span> header — that is SPF, DKIM and DMARC — it does not prove
            the sending domain is related to the PTR hostname, and a patient spammer with their own
            IP block can set it up correctly. Treat it as a floor to clear, not a reputation.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="rdns-prose">
        <h3 class="h3">Why a missing PTR gets your mail deferred</h3>
        <p>
          When a server connects to deliver mail, the receiver looks up the reverse name of the
          connecting IP before the conversation gets very far. In Postfix terms,
          <span class="mono">reject_unknown_reverse_client_hostname</span> rejects a client with no
          PTR at all, and <span class="mono">reject_unknown_client_hostname</span> goes further and
          requires the forward lookup to confirm — the check this page runs. Both default to a
          <span class="mono">450</span> temporary failure rather than a hard
          <span class="mono">550</span>, which is why the symptom is rarely a clean bounce. Your mail
          queue fills with deferrals, retries stretch out over hours, and some of it eventually
          expires. A well-behaved sender is supposed to retry, so you often discover the problem days
          later from a recipient who never got the message.
        </p>
        <p>
          The convention worth following is that three things agree: the PTR name for your IP, the
          name your server announces in <span class="mono">HELO</span>/<span class="mono">EHLO</span>,
          and a forward A or AAAA record for that name pointing back at the same IP. Mismatches
          between the HELO name and the reverse name are a common scoring signal in SpamAssassin and
          in commercial filters, even when each half is individually valid. If you run mail on IPv6,
          set the AAAA reverse record too: several large providers are markedly stricter over IPv6
          than IPv4 and will refuse mail from an IPv6 address with no reverse name even when the IPv4
          path works fine.
        </p>
        <p>
          One practical note on timing. Reverse zones are frequently served with long TTLs — a day is
          common — and negative answers get cached as well. After your provider sets a PTR, this tool
          may keep reporting the old value, or none, until Cloudflare's cache expires. That is a
          caching artefact, not a failed change.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="rdns-limits">
        <div class="rdns-limits-head">
          <div class="icon-tile"><Icon name="pulse" size={20} /></div>
          <h3 class="h3">What this tool cannot tell you</h3>
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>It sees one resolver's view.</strong> Every DNS query goes to Cloudflare's
            public DoH resolver from your browser. A receiving mail server asks its own resolver, and
            can get a different or fresher answer.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>"Use my IP" asks a third party.</strong> A browser cannot see its own external
            address, so that button fetches it from <span class="mono">ipify.org</span> — the one
            request on this page that does not go to Cloudflare. Type the address in by hand if you
            would rather not involve them. Either way, nothing is sent to us.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cached answers can be stale.</strong> Reverse zones often use TTLs of hours to a
            day, and NXDOMAIN is cached too, so a change you just made may not be visible here yet.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It cannot check reputation.</strong> Blocklist membership (Spamhaus PBL/SBL,
            Barracuda, Proofpoint), ASN, and IP history all need server-side APIs. A pass here says
            nothing about whether your IP is listed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It cannot see receiver policy.</strong> Whether a given provider requires
            confirmation, merely scores it, or ignores it is a per-operator decision that is not
            published. Nothing here predicts a specific inbox's verdict.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>It does not test SMTP.</strong> No connection is made to port 25, so the
            <span class="mono">HELO</span> name your server announces — the third part of the
            convention above — cannot be read from a browser and is not checked.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>No PTR is not always a fault.</strong> Web servers, workstations and anything
            that never sends mail are fine without one. Private, loopback and documentation ranges
            have no public reverse delegation at all, and the tool says so when it sees one.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Multiple PTR records are allowed</strong> and are reported individually. The
            check passes if any one of them confirms, which matches how most receivers behave, but a
            receiver that tests only the first answer may still disagree.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ToolLayout>

<style>
  /* ---------------------------------- input --------------------------------- */
  .rdns-bar { display: grid; gap: 0.85rem; }

  .rdns-form {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .rdns-input {
    flex: 1 1 22rem;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    transition: border-color 0.2s var(--ease-out), background 0.2s var(--ease-out);
  }
  .rdns-input::placeholder { color: var(--text-muted); }
  .rdns-input:focus-visible {
    outline: none;
    border-color: var(--accent-cyan);
    background: var(--surface-strong);
  }

  .rdns-examples {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .rdns-chip {
    padding: 0.3rem 0.6rem;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color 0.2s var(--ease-out), border-color 0.2s var(--ease-out);
  }
  .rdns-chip:hover { color: var(--text); border-color: var(--border-strong); }

  /* --------------------------------- results -------------------------------- */
  /* Everything below the .rdns-out wrapper is injected by the script on this
     page, so it never receives Astro's scope attribute — each selector has to
     reach through :global() or it silently matches nothing. */
  .rdns-out { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-idle),
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-working) {
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-working strong) { color: var(--text-soft); font-weight: 600; }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-result) { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-verdict) {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.9rem 1.1rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
    padding: 1.1rem 1.25rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-verdict.is-pass) { border-left-color: var(--accent-cyan); }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-verdict.is-fail) { border-left-color: var(--accent-red); }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-verdict.is-none) { border-left-color: var(--accent-violet); }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-verdict.is-error) { border-left-color: var(--accent-red); }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-badge) {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.65rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    border: 1px solid currentColor;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.is-pass .rdns-badge) { color: var(--accent-cyan); }
  .rdns-out :global(.is-fail .rdns-badge),
  .rdns-out :global(.is-error .rdns-badge) { color: var(--accent-red); }
  .rdns-out :global(.is-none .rdns-badge) { color: var(--accent-violet); }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-vbody) { flex: 1 1 20rem; min-width: 0; }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-vtitle) {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--text);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-vtext) {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-vtext + .rdns-vtext) { margin-top: 0.5rem; }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-vtext code) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.86em;
    color: var(--text);
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  /* Terminal pane: the --term-* palette is fixed dark in both themes. */
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-term) {
    background: var(--term-bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--term-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-term-bar) {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.55rem 0.85rem;
    background: var(--term-bar);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--term-border);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-term-bar i) {
    width: 9px;
    height: 9px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--term-border);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-term-bar span) {
    margin-left: 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--term-text);
    opacity: 0.65;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-term-body) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.05rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    line-height: 1.85;
    color: var(--term-text);
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    word-break: break-all;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.t-cmd) { color: var(--term-cmd); }
  .rdns-out :global(.t-ok) { color: var(--term-ok); }
  .rdns-out :global(.t-accent) { color: var(--term-accent); }
  .rdns-out :global(.t-dim) { color: var(--term-text); opacity: 0.55; }
  /* The fixed terminal palette has no failure colour, and theme tokens are
     tuned for the page background rather than this permanently dark pane. */
  .rdns-out :global(.t-bad) { color: #ff8080; }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-facts) {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--border);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-fact) {
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    background: var(--bg-elev);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-fact dt) {
    margin-bottom: 0.3rem;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-fact dd) {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    color: var(--text);
    word-break: break-all;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-fact dd em) {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 0.25rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.76rem;
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    word-break: normal;
  }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-copy) {
    display: inline-flex;
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-xs);
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-copy:hover) { color: var(--text); border-color: var(--border-strong); }

  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-flags) { display: grid; gap: 0.5rem; }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-flag) {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem;
    font-size: 0.86rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-soft);
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px dashed var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }
  .rdns-out :global(.rdns-flag code) {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.86em;
    color: var(--text);
    word-break: break-all;
  }

  /* -------------------------------- explainer ------------------------------- */
  .rdns-notes-sec .glass-card p {
    margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
  .rdns-notes-sec .mono { color: var(--text); font-size: 0.86em; word-break: break-word; }

  .rdns-prose {
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
  }
  .rdns-prose p {
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }

  .rdns-limits {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    padding: 1.5rem;
    background: var(--bg-elev);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
  }
  .rdns-limits-head {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.85rem;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .rdns-limits-head .h3 { margin: 0; }
  .rdns-limits ul {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .rdns-limits li {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
    position: relative;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
  .rdns-limits li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 0.62em;
    left: 0;
    width: 5px;
    height: 5px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent-violet);
  }
  .rdns-limits strong { color: var(--text); font-weight: 600; }
  .rdns-limits .mono { color: var(--text); font-size: 0.86em; }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .rdns-form .btn { flex: 1 1 auto; justify-content: center; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { runReverseLookup, toPtrName, ipKey, fetchPublicIp } from '../../scripts/reverse-dns.js';
  import { esc } from '../../scripts/tool-ui.js';

  const form = document.getElementById('rdns-form');
  const input = document.getElementById('rdns-input');
  const goBtn = document.getElementById('rdns-go');
  const mineBtn = document.getElementById('rdns-mine');
  const out = document.getElementById('rdns-out');

  // lookup() drives both buttons unconditionally, so they belong in the guard.
  if (form && input && out && goBtn && mineBtn) {
    let busy = false;

    const FAMILY = { 4: 'IPv4', 6: 'IPv6' };

    const ttlText = (s) => {
      if (s == null) return null;
      if (s >= 86400) return `${s} s (${(s / 86400).toFixed(1)} days)`;
      if (s >= 3600) return `${s} s (${Math.round(s / 3600)} hours)`;
      if (s >= 60) return `${s} s (${Math.round(s / 60)} min)`;
      return `${s} s`;
    };

    const line = (cls, text) => `<span class="${cls}">${esc(text)}</span>\n`;

    function transcript(r) {
      const t = r.target;
      let s = '';

      if (r.resolvedFrom) {
        s += line('t-cmd', `$ dig +short ${r.resolvedFrom.host} A AAAA`);
        s += line('t-accent', r.resolvedFrom.addr);
        if (r.resolvedFrom.others.length) {
          s += line('t-dim', `;; also: ${r.resolvedFrom.others.join(' ')} (not checked)`);
        }
      }

      s += line('t-cmd', `$ dig -x ${t.ip}`);
      s += line('t-dim', `;; reverse name: ${t.arpa}`);

      if (!r.hostnames.length) {
        if (r.status === 'resolver') {
          s += line('t-bad', `;; status: ${r.rcodeText} — the resolver could not answer this query`);
          s += line('t-bad', ';; forward-confirmed reverse DNS: could not be determined');
          return s;
        }
        s += line('t-bad', r.nxdomain
          ? ';; status: NXDOMAIN — no PTR record exists for this address'
          : ';; status: NOERROR, but no PTR record was returned');
        s += line('t-bad', ';; forward-confirmed reverse DNS: not possible');
        return s;
      }

      r.hostnames.forEach((h) => {
        s += line('t-accent', `${t.arpa}.  ${r.ttl == null ? '' : r.ttl + '  '}IN  PTR  ${h}.`);
      });

      r.checks.forEach((c) => {
        // Both families are queried: a v6 PTR whose host has only an A record
        // is a real and confusing failure mode, so show what actually exists.
        s += line('t-cmd', `$ dig +short ${c.host} A AAAA`);
        if (c.error) {
          s += line('t-bad', `;; forward lookup failed: ${c.error}`);
        } else if (!c.addrs.length) {
          s += line('t-bad', c.nxdomain
            ? ';; NXDOMAIN — the PTR hostname does not resolve at all'
            : ';; no A or AAAA records for the PTR hostname');
        } else {
          c.addrs.forEach((a) => {
            const hit = ipKey(a) === t.key;
            s += line(hit ? 't-ok' : 't-dim', hit ? `${a}    <-- matches the address queried` : a);
          });
        }
      });

      s += line(r.status === 'pass' ? 't-ok' : 't-bad',
        r.status === 'pass'
          ? ';; forward-confirmed reverse DNS: PASS'
          : r.status === 'unknown'
            ? ';; forward-confirmed reverse DNS: could not be determined'
            : ';; forward-confirmed reverse DNS: FAIL');
      return s;
    }

    function verdict(r) {
      if (r.status === 'pass') {
        const host = r.checks.find((c) => c.match).host;
        return {
          cls: 'is-pass',
          badge: 'Confirmed',
          title: `${r.target.ip} confirms as ${host}`,
          body: [
            `The PTR record points at <code>${esc(host)}</code>, and that hostname resolves back to the same address. This is the match receiving mail servers look for, so the reverse-DNS check will not be the reason mail from this address is refused.`,
            'Worth confirming separately that your mail server announces this same name in its HELO/EHLO greeting — that part cannot be tested from a browser.',
          ],
        };
      }

      // A PTR was returned but nothing could be checked against it. Scoring
      // that as a failure would be a confident verdict on no evidence.
      if (r.status === 'unknown') {
        return {
          cls: 'is-error',
          badge: 'Inconclusive',
          title: 'The PTR record was returned, but it could not be confirmed',
          body: [
            `The reverse lookup gave <code>${esc(r.hostnames.join(', '))}</code>, but the forward query for ${r.hostnames.length > 1 ? 'those names' : 'that name'} did not complete, so there is nothing to compare against ${esc(r.target.ip)}.`,
            'This is a problem with the query, not with your DNS — retry, and check whether a VPN, extension or network policy is interfering with DNS-over-HTTPS requests.',
          ],
        };
      }

      if (r.status === 'fail') {
        // Explain using a check that actually completed; an errored one has
        // nothing to say about why confirmation failed.
        const c = r.checks.find((x) => !x.error) || r.checks[0];
        const why = c.error
          ? `the forward lookup for <code>${esc(c.host)}</code> did not complete`
          : c.nxdomain
            ? `the hostname <code>${esc(c.host)}</code> does not resolve at all`
            : c.addrs.length
              ? `<code>${esc(c.host)}</code> resolves to ${esc(c.addrs.join(', '))} instead`
              : `<code>${esc(c.host)}</code> has no A or AAAA record`;
        return {
          cls: 'is-fail',
          badge: 'Not confirmed',
          title: 'A PTR record exists, but it does not confirm',
          body: [
            `The reverse lookup returned ${r.hostnames.length > 1 ? 'names' : 'a name'}, but the forward lookup does not come back to ${esc(r.target.ip)} — ${why}.`,
            'Receivers treat this as an unconfirmed client. Postfix’s <code>reject_unknown_client_hostname</code> answers with a <code>450</code> temporary failure, so mail is deferred and retried rather than bounced, which makes the problem easy to miss. Fix it by publishing a forward A or AAAA record for the PTR hostname that points back at this address, or by asking the address holder to change the PTR to a name you do control.',
          ],
        };
      }

      // An empty answer only means "no PTR" for NOERROR and NXDOMAIN. On any
      // other rcode the record may well exist; telling the user to go and ask
      // their ISP for one would be advice built on a resolver error.
      if (r.status === 'resolver') {
        return {
          cls: 'is-error',
          badge: 'No answer',
          title: `The resolver returned ${r.rcodeText} for this address`,
          body: [
            `Cloudflare answered <code>${esc(r.rcodeText)}</code> for <code>${esc(r.target.arpa)}</code> instead of returning records. That is a failure to answer, not a missing PTR — this result is no evidence either way.`,
            'Retry in a moment. A SERVFAIL that persists usually means the nameservers for the reverse zone are broken or unreachable, which receiving mail servers will read the same way they read a missing record, so it is still worth raising with whoever runs that zone.',
          ],
        };
      }

      const extra = r.special
        ? `This address is in ${esc(r.special)}, which has no public reverse delegation, so the empty result is expected rather than a misconfiguration.`
        : 'If this address sends mail, this is the single most likely reason its messages are being deferred or rejected. Ask whoever allocated the address — your hosting provider or ISP — to set the PTR record; you cannot publish it from your own domain’s zone.';
      return {
        cls: 'is-none',
        badge: 'No PTR',
        title: `No reverse record for ${r.target.ip}`,
        body: [
          `Nothing answers for <code>${esc(r.target.arpa)}</code>.`,
          extra,
        ],
      };
    }

    function facts(r) {
      const t = r.target;
      const rows = [];

      rows.push([FAMILY[t.family] + ' address', esc(t.ip),
        t.family === 6 && t.expanded !== t.ip ? `expanded: ${esc(t.expanded)}` : null]);

      rows.push(['Reverse name', esc(t.arpa),
        t.family === 6
          ? '32 nibbles of the expanded address, reversed, one per label'
          : 'four octets reversed']);

      rows.push(['PTR record', r.hostnames.length ? esc(r.hostnames.join(', ')) : '—',
        r.hostnames.length > 1 ? `${r.hostnames.length} PTR records returned` : null]);

      rows.push(['TTL', ttlText(r.ttl) ? esc(ttlText(r.ttl)) : '—',
        r.ttl != null && r.ttl >= 3600 ? 'changes can take this long to appear here' : null]);

      rows.push(['Reverse zone', r.zone ? esc(r.zone.zone) : '—',
        r.zone && r.zone.primary
          ? `primary nameserver: ${esc(r.zone.primary)} — whoever runs this controls the PTR`
          : 'no SOA returned for this reverse name']);

      return `<dl class="rdns-facts">${rows
        .map(([k, v, note]) => `<div class="rdns-fact"><dt>${esc(k)}</dt><dd>${v}${
          note ? `<em>${note}</em>` : ''
        }</dd></div>`)
        .join('')}</dl>`;
    }

    function flags(r) {
      const items = [];
      if (r.resolvedFrom) {
        items.push(
          `You entered a hostname, so it was resolved first: <code>${esc(r.resolvedFrom.host)}</code> &rarr; <code>${esc(r.resolvedFrom.addr)}</code>. Everything below is the reverse lookup for that address.` +
            (r.resolvedFrom.others.length
              ? ` It also has ${r.resolvedFrom.others.length} other address${r.resolvedFrom.others.length > 1 ? 'es' : ''}, each with its own PTR record — check them separately.`
              : '')
        );
      }
      if (r.special && r.status !== 'none') {
        items.push(`Note: this address is in ${esc(r.special)}.`);
      }
      if (r.hostnames.length > 1) {
        items.push(
          `${r.hostnames.length} PTR records were returned. That is legal but unusual; the check above passes if any one of them confirms, and a receiver that only tests the first answer may reach a different conclusion.`
        );
      }
      if (!items.length) return '';
      return `<div class="rdns-flags">${items.map((i) => `<p class="rdns-flag">${i}</p>`).join('')}</div>`;
    }

    function render(r) {
      const v = verdict(r);
      out.innerHTML = `
        <div class="rdns-result">
          <div class="rdns-verdict ${v.cls}">
            <span class="rdns-badge">${esc(v.badge)}</span>
            <div class="rdns-vbody">
              <h3 class="rdns-vtitle">${esc(v.title)}</h3>
              ${v.body.map((p) => `<p class="rdns-vtext">${p}</p>`).join('')}
            </div>
          </div>
          ${flags(r)}
          <div class="rdns-term">
            <div class="rdns-term-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>reverse-dns ${esc(r.target.ip)}</span></div>
            <pre class="rdns-term-body"><code>${transcript(r)}</code></pre>
          </div>
          ${facts(r)}
          <div><button type="button" class="rdns-copy" data-copy="${esc(r.target.arpa)}">Copy reverse name</button></div>
        </div>`;
    }

    function renderError(title, hint) {
      out.innerHTML = `
        <div class="rdns-verdict is-error">
          <span class="rdns-badge">Cannot check</span>
          <div class="rdns-vbody">
            <h3 class="rdns-vtitle">${esc(title)}</h3>
            <p class="rdns-vtext">${esc(hint)}</p>
          </div>
        </div>`;
    }

    function renderWorking(raw) {
      const t = toPtrName(raw);
      out.innerHTML = t
        ? `<p class="rdns-working">Querying <strong>${esc(t.arpa)}</strong> for a PTR record&hellip;</p>`
        : `<p class="rdns-working">Resolving <strong>${esc(raw)}</strong> to an address first&hellip;</p>`;
    }

    async function lookup(raw) {
      if (busy) return;
      busy = true;
      out.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'true');
      goBtn.disabled = true;
      mineBtn.disabled = true;
      const label = goBtn.innerHTML;
      goBtn.textContent = 'Checking…';
      renderWorking(raw);

      try {
        render(await runReverseLookup(raw));
      } catch (err) {
        // A blocked DoH request and a typo need different advice, and a thrown
        // TypeError from fetch is the only signal that separates them.
        const net =
          err instanceof TypeError ||
          /failed to fetch|networkerror|load failed|dns query failed/i.test(err.message || '');
        if (net) {
          renderError(
            'Could not reach the DNS resolver',
            'The query to cloudflare-dns.com did not complete. Check your connection, and whether a browser extension, VPN or network policy is blocking DNS-over-HTTPS requests.'
          );
        } else {
          renderError(
            err.message || 'Something went wrong with that lookup.',
            'Enter a bare IPv4 or IPv6 address, or a hostname that resolves to one.'
          );
        }
      } finally {
        busy = false;
        out.removeAttribute('aria-busy');
        goBtn.disabled = false;
        mineBtn.disabled = false;
        goBtn.innerHTML = label;
      }
    }

    form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const raw = input.value.trim();
      if (!raw) {
        renderError(
          'Nothing to look up',
          'Enter a bare IPv4 or IPv6 address, or a hostname that resolves to one.'
        );
        input.focus();
        return;
      }
      lookup(raw);
    });

    document.querySelectorAll('.rdns-chip').forEach((chip) => {
      chip.addEventListener('click', () => {
        input.value = chip.dataset.ip || '';
        lookup(input.value);
      });
    });

    mineBtn.addEventListener('click', async () => {
      if (busy) return;
      // Hold the busy flag across the fetch too, so a submit during it cannot
      // start a lookup that this handler then silently drops — which left the
      // field showing one address and the results describing another.
      busy = true;
      const label = mineBtn.textContent;
      mineBtn.disabled = true;
      goBtn.disabled = true;
      mineBtn.textContent = 'Finding…';
      let ip = null;
      try {
        ip = await fetchPublicIp();
      } catch {
        ip = null;
      } finally {
        busy = false;
        mineBtn.disabled = false;
        goBtn.disabled = false;
        mineBtn.textContent = label;
      }
      if (!ip) {
        renderError(
          'Could not find your public IP address',
          'The request to ipify.org did not complete — a VPN, extension or network policy may be blocking it. Type the address in instead.'
        );
        input.focus();
        return;
      }
      input.value = ip;
      lookup(ip);
    });

    // Delegated because the button is injected with the results.
    out.addEventListener('click', async (e) => {
      const btn = e.target.closest('.rdns-copy');
      if (!btn) return;
      const text = btn.dataset.copy || '';
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
        btn.textContent = 'Copied';
      } catch {
        btn.textContent = 'Select and copy manually';
      }
      setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = 'Copy reverse name'; }, 2000);
    });

    // Deep link: /tools/reverse-dns?ip=8.8.4.4
    const preset = new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('ip');
    if (preset) {
      input.value = preset;
      lookup(preset);
    }
  }
</script>
